body:
We've been able to reproduce this here, but it seems to be caused by something stale in a virtual environment (we're using Anaconda here).

Taking your requirements.txt I can indeed reproduce the failure, and updating all packages and recompiling pyoptsparse does not resolve the issue.

However, if I create a fresh conda environment with the following:
```
    #! /bin/bash
    conda create -n py37 python=3.7;
    conda activate py37

    pip install --upgrade pip;
    pip install numpy;
    pip install scipy;
    pip install mpi4py;
    pip install matplotlib;
    pip install networkx;
    pip install testflo;
    pip install pyyaml;
    cd $OPENMDAO_HOME;
    pip install -e .;
    cd $DYMOS_HOME;
    pip install -e .;
```
where OpenMDAO and DYMOS home directories are cloned from github.

The issue is not longer present.  I can't say at this time exactly what is causing the issue.  It seems like a stale numpy version is in the base environment and is perhaps causing problems.
All the users who started with a fresh conda installation here successfully ran all tests.

@robfalck doesn't this test already check for this: 
https://github.com/OpenMDAO/dymos/blob/master/dymos/examples/cannonball/test/test_connect_control_to_parameter.py

What am I missing here? Seems like this should work, no?

@mcgarnett The recently merged PR #1127 should resolve your issue. Included in it is a simple example showing matrix parameters. The example you provided is somewhat more complicated from a sizing/connections perspective but it should work if you make the two following changes

1. Set `vectorize_A=False` when declaring the linear system comp

2. Set `static_target=True` when adding `A` as a parameter
